{
  "title": "Distal Clavicle Physeal Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4122/distal-clavicle-physeal-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Aug 10 2022",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Distal Clavicle Physeal Fractures are rare injuries to the distal physis of the clavicle in skeletally immature patients.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made with plain radiographs.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally nonoperative management with a sling. Surgical management is indicated for open fractures or those associated with impending soft tissue compromise.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**rare injury**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "accounting for only 5%-10% of clavicle fractures in children",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "fall onto an outstretched extremity or onto side of the shoulder.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "direct blow",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "child abuse (rare cause)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "considered a childhood equivalent to **adult AC separation**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "periosteum usually remains intact with injury",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**clavicle displaces away from physis and periosteal sleeve**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "both of which remain attached to the AC and CC ligaments",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Clavicle osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "S-shaped bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "medial clavicle is connected to the axial skeleton via the sternoclavicular joint",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "lateral clavicle is connected to the scapula via the acromioclavicular joint",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Clavicle ossification",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "overview",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**first bone to ossify** in the fifth week in utero",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**physes are the last to close**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "central clavicle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "initial growth via intramembranous ossification from the ossification center in the central portion of the clavicle (<5 years)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "distal clavicle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "continued growth via secondary ossification at lateral physis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "lateral epiphysis does not ossify until age 18 years",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "medial clavicle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "approximately 80% of clavicular growth occurs at the medial physis (secondary ossification)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "medial epiphysis does not begin to ossify until 18 to 20 years",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**last physis to close in body (20-25yrs)**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "thus sternoclavicular dislocations in teenagers/young adults are usually physeal fracture-dislocations",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Coracoclavicular (CC) ligaments",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "provide vertical stability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "composed of",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "trapezoid ligaments",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "2 cm from AC joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "conoid ligaments",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "4 cm from AC joint",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Acromioclavicular (AC) ligaments",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "provide horizontal stability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
